CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 2 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
`with_index_under_if` (used by `access_offset()` for the read-side `where`
computation) recognises `with`/`index` directly nested under the #305
pointer-identity if-chain, and recurses through further nested `if`s -- but
reading `arr[k]` inside an `if`-condition (`if (arr[k] != 0) ...`), rather
than materialising it into a plain temporary first, folds the dereference
straight into the boolean test instead: `!(arr[k] == 0)`. Two separate gaps
combined to leave this at the sentinel:

1. `with_index_under_if` had no case for `ID_not`/`ID_equal`/`ID_notequal`,
   so it never looked inside the comparison to find the nested index.
2. Even after adding that, the `if`-chain's own operand-skip
   (`op.type().id() == ID_bool`, meant to skip only the if's condition) was
   wrong here: when the if itself computes a *boolean* result (as happens
   one level up, in a guard update), the true/false branches are bool-typed
   too and were wrongly skipped along with the condition. Fixed to skip by
   position (always operand 0) instead of by type.
3. A read consumed by an if-condition also has a `GOTO` step (the branch
   instruction itself) between the read and the assignment that computes
   the guard, where the generic write/read site code only ever looked at
   the immediate next step.

Reproducer: one thread's `pthread_create` writes a symbolic-size malloc'd
`pthread_t` array element, another reads it inside `if (tids[0] != 0)`,
protected by a mutex only on the reading side (so still racy against the
unsynchronised write). Reported SUCCESSFUL before this fix (the read never
matched the write's location), FAILED after.

See `if_condition_read_race_ordered`, the negative control: same shape but
the write happens-before the reader thread is created, so there is no race
-- must stay SUCCESSFUL before and after.
